{"statement":"For $N\ge 1$ let $[N]=\{1,\dots,N\}$ and let $s(N)$ be the largest size of a set $A\subseteq[N]$ containing no triple $x,\;x+d^2,\;x+2d^2$ with $x,d\in\mathbb N$, $d\ge1$ (a non-trivial $3$-term arithmetic progression whose common difference is a perfect square). Let $r_3(N)$ be the largest size of a subset of $[N]$ with no non-trivial $3$-term progression; trivially $s(N)\ge r_3(N)\ge N\exp(-c\sqrt{\log N})$ (Behrend), while the best known upper bound is of the form $s(N)\ll N(\log\log N)^{-c}$.

(1) Does there exist an absolute constant $c>0$ such that $s(N)\ll N\exp\!\big(-(\log N)^{c}\big)$, i.e. does a Kelley–Meka-type bound hold when the common difference is restricted to squares?

(2) Is $\log\big(N/s(N)\big)\asymp\log\big(N/r_3(N)\big)$, or can sets avoiding square-difference progressions be substantially denser than $3$-AP-free sets, e.g. $s(N)\ge N\exp(-(\log N)^{1/2-c})$ for some $c>0$?","grounding":"The problem extends "Result: Strong bounds for 3-term progressions" (Kelley–Meka): their theorem gives $r_3(N)\ll N\exp(-(\log N)^{\beta})$ for unrestricted common differences. The gap is the restriction of the common difference to a sparse structured set: for the two-point configuration $x,x+d^2$ (Furstenberg–Sárközy) Kelley–Meka-type bounds $N\exp(-(\log N)^c)$ are known (Green–Sawhney, 2024), and for the three-point configuration $x,x+d^2,x+2d^2$ only $(\log\log N)^{-c}$-type bounds (Prendiville's quantitative homogeneous polynomial Szemerédi) are available. Nothing in the Kelley–Meka section or its finite-field model addresses sparse difference sets, and no lower bound better than the trivial $s(N)\ge r_3(N)$ is known. It also connects to "Result: Improved bounds for Szemerédi's theorem" (Leng–Sah–Sawhney), whose methods give $\exp(-(\log\log N)^{c})$-type bounds for higher-complexity patterns but nothing of Kelley–Meka strength.","why_hard":"(a) The Kelley–Meka sifting/almost-periodicity argument controls $\sum_{x,d}1_A(x)1_A(x+d)1_A(x+2d)$ through the convolutions $1_A*1_A$ and $1_A*1_{-A}$ averaged over the whole group; when $d$ is restricted to the squares $Q\subseteq[N]$, of density $N^{-1/2}$, the count becomes a sparse bilinear average $\sum_x 1_A(x)\sum_{q\in Q}1_A(x+q)1_A(x+2q)$ and there is no known analogue of almost periodicity of $1_A*1_A$ along a sparse polynomial set. (b) The finite-field model that Kelley–Meka use to develop their method degenerates: in $\mathbb F_p^n$ the squares have density about $1/2$, so the model problem is essentially the ordinary Roth problem and offers no guidance for sparsity. (c) The Fourier/circle-method route (Green, Prendiville) must handle the major arcs of $\widehat{1_Q}$; squares are badly distributed modulo small primes, forcing a $W$-trick and a density increment on progressions $\{a+q^2 m\}$ whose modulus grows at every step, which is exactly where the $\log\log N$ loss arises. (d) Peluse's degree-lowering gives polylogarithmic bounds for $x,x+y,x+y^2$ but its iterative structure is inherently incompatible with the single-step quasipolynomial gain in Kelley–Meka. (e) For the lower bound, Ruzsa-type constructions for $x,x+d^2$ give $N^{1-c}$, far below Behrend; no mechanism is known to exploit the sparsity of squares to beat $r_3(N)$ for three-point patterns.","why_important":"A positive answer to (1) would be the first Kelley–Meka-strength bound for a genuinely polynomial configuration of length three, bridging the new quasipolynomial technology with the polynomial Szemerédi theorem of Bergelson–Leibman, and would almost certainly require a new "sparse almost periodicity" principle applicable to other polynomial patterns ($x,x+d^k,x+2d^k$, progressions with prime-minus-one differences). The lower-bound question (2) tests whether Behrend-type constructions are optimal even in the presence of a sparse difference set; a construction beating Behrend would be a new type of example in additive combinatorics, whereas a proof that $\log(N/s(N))\asymp\log(N/r_3(N))$ would show the restriction to squares is quantitatively harmless, in sharp contrast to the two-point case where Ruzsa's $N^{1-c}$ example shows the opposite.","resembles_known":"Polynomial Szemerédi theorem (Bergelson–Leibman) in its quantitative form; Furstenberg–Sárközy theorem; Roth's theorem / Kelley–Meka bound.","novelty_confidence":0.55}
